1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:01,872
Friday, eleven at night.

2
00:00:01,922 --> 00:00:04,104
A call reaches Timur's card zone.

3
00:00:04,154 --> 00:00:07,704
Its header says: I am ecom, charge this card.

4
00:00:07,754 --> 00:00:09,576
Anyone can type that line.

5
00:00:09,626 --> 00:00:12,336
A name you write yourself proves nothing.

6
00:00:12,386 --> 00:00:18,408
In Lesson 6, every key came from Vault, and a service with a missing setting refused to start.

7
00:00:18,458 --> 00:00:24,720
This lesson uses those keys to prove, on every call, that the caller really is ecom.

8
00:00:24,770 --> 00:00:27,912
Timur gets into the bank every morning the same way.

9
00:00:27,962 --> 00:00:31,392
He shows an ID card from the building's own pass office.

10
00:00:31,442 --> 00:00:37,728
Reception gives him a day pass that lists the floors he may visit, and the lift goes only to those floors.

11
00:00:37,778 --> 00:00:43,872
The card zone checks every call like that: two proofs, from two places, and a list of floors.

12
00:00:43,922 --> 00:00:50,184
The first proof is a certificate: a digital ID card, signed by the card zone's own pass office.

13
00:00:50,234 --> 00:00:53,232
When a caller connects, it shows its certificate.

14
00:00:53,282 --> 00:00:55,968
The card zone shows its own certificate back.

15
00:00:56,018 --> 00:00:58,440
So each side knows who is on the other end.

16
00:00:58,490 --> 00:01:02,784
Because both sides show one, this is called mutual TLS.

17
00:01:02,834 --> 00:01:09,960
If a caller has no certificate from the card zone's pass office, the connection is refused before any request is even read.

18
00:01:10,010 --> 00:01:13,584
The second proof is an access token: the day pass.

19
00:01:13,634 --> 00:01:18,624
The caller asks for it from the card zone's own login server, using its own secret.

20
00:01:18,674 --> 00:01:21,384
The card zone trusts only that login server.

21
00:01:21,434 --> 00:01:24,960
A pass from another product's login server is worth nothing here.

22
00:01:25,010 --> 00:01:28,752
Inside the access token is who it belongs to, and its roles.

23
00:01:28,802 --> 00:01:31,920
A role is a floor: one kind of operation.

24
00:01:31,970 --> 00:01:34,200
One role lets a caller bind a card.

25
00:01:34,250 --> 00:01:36,072
Another lets it read a card.

26
00:01:36,122 --> 00:01:37,944
Another lets it charge a card.

27
00:01:37,994 --> 00:01:42,768
Every operation needs its own role, and anything not listed is refused.

28
00:01:42,818 --> 00:01:46,152
So even the real ecom can only do what its roles allow.

29
00:01:46,202 --> 00:01:50,616
No caller at all holds the role for the card zone's admin operations.

30
00:01:50,666 --> 00:01:52,968
Many teams stop at the first proof.

31
00:01:53,018 --> 00:01:57,624
I think one proof is never enough: steal one file, and you are in.

32
00:01:57,674 --> 00:02:02,112
These checks have no screen, so read Timur's log for that Friday night.

33
00:02:02,162 --> 00:02:05,136
A caller with no certificate knocks on the partner door.

34
00:02:05,186 --> 00:02:07,128
The connection closes at once.

35
00:02:07,178 --> 00:02:09,984
No request is read, and nothing is answered.

36
00:02:10,034 --> 00:02:14,784
Next, a caller brings a valid certificate, but a pass from the wrong login server.

37
00:02:14,834 --> 00:02:17,520
The card zone answers: unauthorized.

38
00:02:17,570 --> 00:02:21,384
The pass did not come from its own login server, so it counts for nothing.

39
00:02:21,434 --> 00:02:24,384
Then the real ecom calls, with both proofs.

40
00:02:24,434 --> 00:02:28,272
It asks to charge a buyer's card by its token, for Malika's shop.

41
00:02:28,322 --> 00:02:32,304
Its pass carries the role to charge, so the call goes through.

42
00:02:32,354 --> 00:02:36,528
Then ecom asks for an admin operation, one that searches cards.

43
00:02:36,578 --> 00:02:38,400
Both proofs pass.

44
00:02:38,450 --> 00:02:43,104
But ecom's pass has no admin role, so the card zone answers: forbidden.

45
00:02:43,154 --> 00:02:46,104
Three refusals, for three different reasons.

46
00:02:46,154 --> 00:02:48,480
No certificate means no connection.

47
00:02:48,530 --> 00:02:50,856
A bad pass means unauthorized.

48
00:02:50,906 --> 00:02:52,848
A missing role means forbidden.

49
00:02:52,898 --> 00:02:56,496
Each answer tells Timur exactly which check stopped the call.

50
00:02:56,546 --> 00:03:00,504
The card zone keeps its own pass office and its own login server.

51
00:03:00,554 --> 00:03:05,568
Neither is shared with ecom, so nothing outside the room can hand out the room's passes.

52
00:03:05,618 --> 00:03:09,120
So steal only the certificate, and you have no pass.

53
00:03:09,170 --> 00:03:12,624
Steal only the pass, and you cannot even connect.

54
00:03:12,674 --> 00:03:18,408
Steal both from ecom, and you still reach only ecom's floors, never the admin floor.

55
00:03:18,458 --> 00:03:25,200
And a mistake inside ecom cannot give ecom more power, because only the card zone grants the roles.

56
00:03:25,250 --> 00:03:33,216
Saturday morning, Timur reads the log: three refusals and one charge, and each says exactly where it stopped.

57
00:03:33,266 --> 00:03:36,672
Next: how the card zone talks to every card network.
